research/audit/wave8-real-analysis-cantor-continuity.provenance.jsonl-62-{"id":"cex-x-squared-is-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniform_continuity"],"rationale":"The source gives x squared on the real line as a standard continuous but not uniformly continuous example. The local item fixes explicit pairs compatible with zero-based indexing.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-real-analysis-cantor-continuity.provenance.jsonl-63-{"id":"ex-x-to-the-beta-separates-the-holder-classes","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://zaguan.unizar.es/record/125531/files/TAZ-TFG-2022-2652.pdf","https://people.math.wisc.edu/~angenent/521.2016s/index.html"],"rationale":"The university thesis proves that x to the alpha is alpha-Hölder, while the Wisconsin exercise uses power functions to separate Hölder orders. The local item combines these into the precise rational-exponent noninclusion statement.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave8-real-analysis-cantor-continuity.provenance.jsonl:64:{"id":"cex-product-of-uniformly-continuous-functions-need-not-be-uniformly-continuous","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~sussmann/exam-311-April-30-2018-answers.pdf"],"rationale":"The Rutgers examination solutions give exactly f(x)=g(x)=x on the real line as uniformly continuous factors whose product x squared is not uniformly continuous. This live source supersedes the inaccessible Johns Hopkins Spring 2019 solutions URL.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
