research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/cex-x-squared-is-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r--e4fd9e3298f77c70.prompt.md-778-
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/cex-x-squared-is-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r--e4fd9e3298f77c70.prompt.md-779-- **What this witnesses in the regularity hierarchy.** [[thm-metric-regularity-hierarchy]], transported to real functions by [[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]], gives uniformly continuous $\Rightarrow$ continuous and asserts no converse. This item is the witness that the converse fails, and it is one of the two named in the remarks of [[lem-real-and-metric-notions-agree]]; the other, [[ex-x-to-the-beta-separates-the-holder-classes]], separates the Hölder classes below it.
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/cex-x-squared-is-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r--e4fd9e3298f77c70.prompt.md-780-
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/cex-x-squared-is-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r--e4fd9e3298f77c70.prompt.md-781-- **The failure is at the missing endpoint, and it is repaired by restoring it.** On $[a,1]$ with $0 < a < 1$ the same formula is uniformly continuous, by [[thm-heine-cantor-r]], since $[a,1]$ is closed and bounded. It is also repaired by an explicit estimate: on $[a,1]$ one has $|1/x - 1/x'| = |x - x'|/(xx') \le |x-x'|/a^{2}$, so $f$ is even Lipschitz there.
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/cex-x-squared-is-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r--e4fd9e3298f77c70.prompt.md-782-
research/audit/wave8-preserved-refuters/cex-x-squared-is-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r--e4fd9e3298f77c70.prompt.md-783-- **A second reading of the same fact.** By [[cor-continuous-extension-from-a-dense-subset-r]], a uniformly continuous function on $(0,1)$ would extend continuously to $[0,1]$ and hence be bounded there ([[cor-boundedness-theorem-r]]); $1/x$ is unbounded on $(0,1)$, so it cannot be uniformly continuous. That route is shorter but rests on more, and the computation above is the elementary one.
