research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-225964-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-225965-- **The witness is as concrete as it can be here.** For $\beta = 1/2$ the function is $\sqrt{x}$, and the failure of the Lipschitz condition is the familiar one: $|\sqrt{x} - \sqrt{0}| = \sqrt{x}$ exceeds $C x$ for every $C$ once $x < 1/C^{2}$. Step 2.2 is that computation written for a general rational exponent.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-225966-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-225967-- **Subadditivity is the whole of claim 1**, and it is proved by normalising to $p + q = 1$ and using $t^{\beta} \ge t$ on $[0,1]$. No derivative and no convexity argument is used; neither is available at this point in the reading order.
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-225968-
research/audit/wave8-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-cantor-continuity-A4.log-225969-- **What happens at the two ends of the range.** At $\beta = 1$ the function is the identity, Lipschitz and not $\alpha$-Hölder for any rational $\alpha > 1$ — indeed no nonconstant function is, by [[thm-holder-exponent-above-one-forces-constancy]], which is why [[def-lipschitz-holder-contraction]] stops at $1$. As $\beta$ decreases the class grows, and claim 4 says it grows strictly at every rational step.
